To calculate the price of a three-year bond with 5% coupon rate and a yield of 6%, we need the current face value of the bond, which is not provided. To compute the price of the bond, we typically use the bond yield formula, which is:
\[ \text{Price\} = \frac{F}{(1 + \text{Coupon\ Rate\} \times \text{Years\})} \text{Cash\} \]
Where \( F \) is the face value, \( \text{Payments\} \) is the coupon rate as a decimal, \( \text{Cash\} \) is the cash flow per year.
Given the coupon rate is 5% (5/1000) and the certificate of noting (cash flow per year) is 5%, the calculation would be as follows:
\[ \text{Cash\} = \text{Notes\} - \left(\frac{5\}{{1 + 0.05 \times \text{Years\}}\times \text{Years\}\right) \right) \]
